# Issue #689: A `Vec[String]` local given views becomes `Vec[ref String]` silently; NK1102 appears at a later call, not where the views went in

[Open and answer this issue on GitHub](https://github.com/Nikaia-Foundation/Nikaia/issues/689).

`compiler`, `ready` · opened 2026-10-19 · updated 2026-10-19 · 0 comments

Found while writing the 1BRC measurement harness in Nikaia.

## Probe

```nika
struct B { args: Vec[String] }

fn make(args: Vec[String]) -> B {
    let mut all: Vec[String] = ["build"]
    for a in args { all.push(a) }
    return B { args: all }
}

fn show(xs: ref Vec[String]) -> String {
    return xs.join(" ")
}

fn main() {
    let b = make(["--release"])
    println(show(b.args))
}
```

`nikaia run` (develop, bce01f8) refuses this with NK1102 at `show(b.args)` (line 22): "expects `ref Vec[String]`, but you're passing `Vec[ref String]`; help: Make it a `ref Vec[String]`, or change the declaration to `Vec[ref String]`".

## Why this is a defect

Every type in this program is written `String`. Under ADR-282 D2 the compiler decides where text is a view, so the program is not wrong. The tier walk lets `all` and the field `B.args` hold views, because of what flows into them. It does not carry that decision on to `show`'s parameter, so the mismatch only surfaces at a call far from where it starts. The help text then suggests writing `ref String` in a declaration, which CLAUDE.md's rules forbid in that position. Writing `all.push(a.clone())` makes it build, at the cost of a copy nobody asked for.

## To build

1. The tier walk decides `show(xs: ref Vec[String])`'s element tier from what its callers pass, as it already does for a `String` parameter. A view-holding `B.args` then lowers `show` over `&[&str]`, or over `EitherText` where both kinds arrive. Alternatively, where the walk decides the field must own its text, convert at the `push`. Do not refuse at the call.
2. If a refusal is still right in some case (a `pub` boundary), it is reported where the views enter the `Vec[String]`, and the help does not suggest `ref String` in a declaration.
3. Test: the probe runs and prints `build --release`. A `pub fn show` variant is covered too, with its rule.

